sections.body:
**Supreme audit institutions** are an important link in the chain of accountability. They give independent reviews of government finances and performance to parliaments and the public. INTOSAI, the International Organization of Supreme Audit Institutions, lists its member bodies and has published guidelines for auditing PPPs.

Their mandate should generally include **two levels of audit**:

| Type | What it checks | PPP focus |
|---|---|---|
| **Regularity audit** | Financial statements of government entities and of government as a whole; compliance and probity of decision-making | PPP commitments are correctly reflected in the accounts and PPP processes were followed; can happen at any stage |
| **Performance (value for money) audit** | Effectiveness and efficiency of government | Whether a project or program gives value for money, or whether processes were properly followed; at project or program level |

**The access problem.** Audit mandates usually cover only government agencies and entities wholly or mostly owned by government. So auditors typically **have no right to audit the PPP company**, even though it holds much of the relevant information, and attempts to get that information can cause conflict. The solution is contractual: the **PPP contract should require the company to provide audited accounts and any other data the government may require**, and the audit entity should be clear about its rights to access that information.

**When to audit.** INTOSAI recommends a performance audit **soon after procurement**, with further reviews over the project life. These should cover:
- all major aspects of the deal that affect value for money, such as required actions, outputs and delivery timing;
- how the PPP was identified;
- how the transaction process was managed;
- the tender process adopted;
- how the contract was finalised;
- ongoing management of the contract.
